TEF — EPE‑04‑K1

Family: EPE‑04 (MSEPE — Membrane‑Sealed Excision Plate Engineering)
Subtype: Foil‑Membrane Sealed Plate
Event Date: 13 April 2026
Preparation Date: ~1 week prior
Substrate

Brown, double‑sided, slightly bendable oval placemat with a smooth upper surface and flexible composite body.
Materials

    Tacky glue (single uniform coat)

    Giftwrap foil (three‑layer laminate: PET carrier → metallic foil → tint layer)

    Foil placed face‑down as pressing surface

Procedure

    A single coat of tacky glue was applied to the placemat surface.

    Giftwrap foil was placed face‑down onto the wet glue, functioning initially as a SLEP‑style pressing sheet.

    The plate was left to cure for approximately one week.

    Today, the PET carrier sheet was peeled back to assess adhesion and layer behaviour.

    A small test incision was made into the resulting surface to evaluate excision behaviour.

Observations

    The clear PET carrier lifted off in one intact membrane.

    The metallic foil + tint layer separated from the PET and remained fully sealed into the tacky‑glue skin.

    The separation was clean, with no tearing or partial transfer.

    The resulting surface is a metallic‑tint composite skin bonded to the placemat, with the PET acting as a sacrificial membrane.

    A small incision cut into the surface excised successfully, removing only the engineered skin layer and exposing the ink‑amenable lower glue layer without damaging the substrate.

    This confirms that the plate is structurally excisable and behaves as a functional MSEP, pending inking tests.

Interpretation

This test began as a SLEP‑style pressing experiment but revealed itself as a true membrane‑sealed event when the PET carrier released cleanly, leaving the functional metallic+tint layer sealed beneath. The giftwrap foil’s internal structure (PET → foil → tint) enabled a selective transfer identical in logic to TEMSEP and IEMSEP behaviour: the membrane releases, the functional layer remains embedded.

The successful incision demonstrates that the sealed metallic‑tint skin behaves as a cuttable, non‑destructive excision surface, with a predictable failure plane and direct access to the ink‑receiving layer. This elevates EPE‑04‑K1 from a structural curiosity to a fully functional MSEP prototype.
Lineage Notes

    Ancestor logic: TEMSEP/IEMSEP membrane‑release behaviour.

    Sibling logic: Other EPE‑04 membrane‑sealed plates using sacrificial carriers.

    Descendant potential:

        Foil‑membrane variants with controlled tint thickness

        Multi‑layer metallic membranes

        Pressure‑modulated foil‑transfer MSEPs

        Hybrid MSEP/SLEP plates with engineered incision depth

